title: Forgetting a volume
tags: [[Mount points]]
kind: guide
summary: mf mount lists the mount points and their state; mf mount forget un-declares one that is no longer a mount point.

```
mf -n media mount                       # mount list
online     /media/backup   uuid:1234-ABCD  uuid:1234-ABCD
offline    /media/photos   label:PHOTOS    -
mf -n media mount forget 3f2a…          # asks for confirmation; -y does not
```

`mf mount list` (the default) prints `state  path  expected  current` per mount point,
or a "no mount points" line; `--json` the raw answer ([[Mount status]]).

`mf mount forget <uuid>` un-declares a mount point: a forced unset of `mfr_mount`. The
directory becomes an ordinary tracked directory again — so if nothing is mounted there,
its former contents stop being frozen, and the next orphan detection will rightly report
them as gone ([[Finding and clearing orphans]]). It is the escape hatch for "this is not a mount point any more".

There is no `mf mount add`: mount points are detected. Mount the volume and run a
reconcile to record one; after plugging a known one back in, reconcile its subtree to
catch up (`mf reconcile --metarecord <uuid>`).
